## Key Findings

### 1. Parts risk: {{stockout_bins}} bins at zero, {{below_reorder_bins}} more below reorder

{{tools_with_stockout}} of {{total_tools}} tools carry at least one
stocked-out part on their bill of materials, meaning a single unexpected
failure now has no spare to replace it with. Replenishment lead times matter
here: stocked-out parts with a lead time of 45+ days account for
{{n_long_lead}} of the zero-stock bins and touch {{tools_with_longlead}}
tools.
*Chart reference: Excel tracker → Reorder Tracker sheet; Tableau worksheet
"Days of Supply Distribution".*

### 2. Training coverage: {{coverage_pct}} of cells meet target, shift D at {{shift_d_ratio}}

Coverage is uneven across the schedule. Shift A averages {{shift_a_ratio}}
coverage versus {{shift_d_ratio}} on shift D, where {{d_uncovered_cells}}
tool-type/site cells have no certified technician at all and {{spof_cells}}
cells rely on a single technician (single point of failure). The thinnest
tool families are {{thin1_type}} ({{thin1_ratio}}) and {{thin2_type}}
({{thin2_ratio}}). {{expiring_90d}} certifications expire within 90 days,
and {{stale_trainings}} in-progress enrollments have been sitting
incomplete for more than 120 days.
*Chart reference: Excel tracker → Training Tracker sheet; Tableau worksheet
"Cert Coverage by Shift".*

### 3. Combined readiness: {{critical_count}} critical, {{at_risk_count}} at-risk

Blending parts and training risk per tool (55/45, weighted by criticality),
{{flagged_count}} tools land below the 55-point readiness line. The worst
concentrations are in the {{thin1_type}} and {{thin2_type}} fleets, where
thin certification coverage compounds with spare-parts exposure.
*Chart reference: Excel tracker → At-Risk Tools sheet; Tableau worksheets
"At-Risk Tools" and "Readiness Heatmap".*

